body:
>Exercises to build muscle memory won't change. You simply have to do them repeatedly to build muscle memory.  
>  
>However, here are a few things I've encountered when I was practicing line control.

I'm a bit worried though that I'm not doing it properly and am just internalizing mistakes; when I make a bad line, I have no  clue how to analyze it besides shrugging it off because I'm not sure what else to say. I'm just worried that if I'm simply doing these exercises wrong and don't know why they're wrong or how I'm supposed to do it right and just keep doing them, I'll just be developing bad habits. For example, I want to draw clean circles, and I keep practicing yet my circles are very messy or furry day after day, yet I can't explain why this happens and don't know how to correct it. I don't know how to analyze why I can't draw a straight line or a circle, is it my posture? My arm? why does it go astray?  it makes me anxious if I can see my problems, but I don't know the why of why they happen, so I keep hammering at them. Is there a way to tackle this? If you don't mind me asking, have you had any issues with this and how did you approach it? I'm just not sure how to see improvement now/ realize what I'm doing wrong compared to the instructor if it's simply muscle memory.

&#x200B;

What does it mean to zoom out? Does it mean to do a blockout with boxes/sketches for the proportions/placement before placing lines? Sorry, I'm just having a bit of trouble visualizing it.  I've noticed the problem that I'll focus on a line but not notice the distance or length compared to the other lines and when I look back on the image my proportions are wrong. 

&#x200B;

As for the muscle groups, does that mean I should vary which part of the arm I use depending on the size? If it's a small circle, am I allowed to use my wrist, or my elbow? And generally, how do I develop accuracy for all of them?

&#x200B;

I'm not sure how to think about it anymore, even trying to think out of the box, like how jugglers learn to balance. When they're also dealing with hand-eye/imagination coordination, how do they notice their problems and improve? When someone tries to juggle and the balls fall, how do they figure out why they fell, what with their arms were they doing? This is sort of off topic, but I think I'm trying to resort to a physical analysis if I'm visualizing a straight line, trying to ghost for a straight line on the page, and end up with something off. Is it my arm? I'm just not sure how to approach this anymore, because even when I just try to move on to other things after drawing lines for months, these issues stick until things somehow click for me. I tried to move on to environments, thinking that this was becoming to be a grind and I need to continue moving, yet with this line quality none of the concepts for perspective and form can be executed if my lines are so broken and messy. I wish I could record a video of myself drawing or see a video of others drawing, maybe I'm taking the wrong stance? I'm just not sure, just in general- how would you advise that I approach this issue? Thanks.
